tations of existing graph DFM models. Sampling complexity. Graph DFM models often rely on sophisticated sampling procedures, including predictor-corrector updates ( Campbell et al., 2022 ) , target guidance and additional stochasticity ( Campbell et al., 2024 ) , as well as carefully designed sampling-time distortions ( Qin et al., 2025 ) . In particular, Luo et al. (2026) ; Qin et al. (2025) design non-uniform time discretizations that concentrate computation in specific regions of the generation trajectory where graph validity is most sensitive. While effective, these techniques introduce additional hyperparameters and design choices, requiring substantial inference-time tuning for each task and sampling budget. Sampling efficiency. DFM unlocks flexible sampling strategies by decoupling model training from sampling. However, existing graph DFM methods still require a large number of function evaluations (NFE), often comparable to that of diffusion-based approaches. In practice, DFM methods simulate the process in Eq. 11 using finite-step approximations such as τ 	au -leaping ( Gillespie, 2001 ) , which hold transition rates fixed over a finite interval and apply the resulting component updates simultaneously. These fixed rates neglect interactions between updates within the interval, thereby introducing approximation errors when multiple graph components change. These errors are more pronounced in the low-step regime, where larger intervals tend to produce more simultaneous updates. Accurately coordinating node and edge transitions is therefore crucial for maintaining sample quality at low NFE. 4 Hazard-Guided Sampling using H-Glow We introduce H-Glow , a novel hazard-guided sampler for graph generation that builds on the decomposition of CTMC generators into hazards and conditional jump distributions. We first instantiate the decomposition for graphs in Section 4.1 , then present our H-Glow sampler in Section 4.2 . Notations. We represent an undirected graph with